package closure_elided_return_annotation_region_admits;
fn go<'a, 'b>(x: &'a i64, y: &'b i64) -> &'a i64 {
    let f = || -> &i64 { return y; };
    return f();
}
fn main() -> i32 {
    let a: i64 = 2i64;
    let b: i64 = 5i64;
    return *go(&a, &b) as i32;
}
// SOUNDNESS QUEUE row closure_elided_return_annotation_region_admits (tier 2).
// Illegal: the closure returns `y: &'b i64`; calling it yields a `&'b i64` returned as `&'a i64` with no `'b: 'a`
// (rustc: lifetime may not live long enough). TODAY admitted. The closure's WRITTEN return type `&i64` has an elided
// region, the call `f()` is typed from that annotation, and an elided sub region against a named return answers
// permissively; the region the body actually returns never reaches the caller. The struct spelling
// (`|| -> P { return P { x: y, y: y }; }` returned as `P<'a>`) is admitted the same way.
